# BrewPlanner server secrets. Copy to /etc/brewplanner.env on the Pi, fill in
# real values, then: sudo chmod 600 /etc/brewplanner.env
#
# This file is referenced by checklist-server.service (EnvironmentFile=).
# NEVER commit the filled-in version to git.

# Signs session cookies. Use a long random string (>= 32 chars), e.g.:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# If unset, the server generates and persists one in data/session-secret.
SESSION_SECRET=

# The initial admin account, created on first boot only if no users exist yet.
# After first boot you can rotate it with:  npm run user -- <name> <password>
ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Read-only Bearer token for headless clients that can't hold a session cookie
# (e.g. the Garmin watch app). When set, requests with
# `Authorization: Bearer <token>` are allowed through the auth guard with the
# same read access as a logged-in user. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# Leave blank to disable this access path entirely.
WATCH_API_TOKEN=

# Optional Brewer's Friend read-only API key. Used only for the one-way legacy
# recipe import; the library itself lives in BrewPlanner's SQLite database and
# all new recipes and edits are local. Imported recipes keep their original URL.
# Generate it under Brewer's Friend → Profile → Integrations (requires an
# account with API access). Leave blank on a fresh BrewPlanner-only setup.
BREWERS_FRIEND_API_KEY=

# Lets the desktop dashboard edit keg contents (the Pi kiosk keg screen stays
# read-only). The keg inventory is a published, read-only Google Sheet, so writes
# go through a Google Apps Script web app deployed on that sheet. Paste the script
# from apps/server/google-apps-script/keg-updater.gs into the sheet
# (Extensions → Apps Script), deploy it as a Web app (Execute as: Me, Access:
# Anyone), and put the /exec deployment URL here. Leave blank to keep kegs
# read-only everywhere (the editor then reports "not configured").
KEG_SHEET_WRITE_URL=

# Brewery speaker (Sonos / IKEA SYMFONISK) for the kiosk now-playing screen. The
# SYMFONISK runs Sonos firmware, so the server controls it directly over the LAN
# — no Spotify account or OAuth. Both are optional:
#   - SONOS_HOST: the speaker's LAN IP. Most reliable (skips multicast discovery
#     entirely); set this if you have more than one Sonos or discovery is flaky.
#   - SONOS_ROOM: the zone/room name (as shown in the Sonos app, e.g. "Brewery").
#     Used to pick the right speaker when discovering. Ignored if SONOS_HOST is set.
# Leave both blank to auto-discover and use the first Sonos that answers.
# SONOS_HOST=192.168.1.50
# SONOS_ROOM=Brewery

# The brewing rig (the separate brew-system-v3 Pi) for the Brew System page.
# Point this at the rig's FastAPI on the LAN — give that Pi a static IP or DHCP
# reservation first so the address doesn't rot. The rig's API is unauthenticated
# by design (LAN-only); this server proxies it behind login + admin role.
# Leave blank to disable — the Brew System page then shows "not configured".
# BREW_SYSTEM_URL=http://192.168.1.60:8000
#
# Settings → Maintenance also has an "Update brew system" button, which SSHes
# into the rig to pull + rebuild + restart it (deploy/update-brew-system.sh).
# That reuses the host above with the `pi` account, so normally you set nothing
# here — you only need to authorise a key once:
#   sudo -u brewplanner ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~brewplanner/.ssh/id_ed25519 -N ''
#   # then append that .pub to the rig's ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
# Override only if the SSH user or host differs from the API host:
# BREW_SYSTEM_SSH_USER=pi
# BREW_SYSTEM_SSH=pi@192.168.1.60

# Telegram notifications (keg too old, fermentation complete). Both must be set
# or the notification scheduler stays disabled. Get the token from @BotFather;
# find the chat id by messaging your bot, then `npm run notify -- chat-id`.
# Test delivery from Settings → Notifications, or `npm run notify -- test`.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=
# Optional: how often (seconds) the scheduler checks the conditions.
# NOTIFY_INTERVAL_SECONDS=300

# --- Bruce, the voice assistant (deploy/bruce.service; see deploy/README-bruce.md)
# This is the only key Bruce needs; everything else has sane defaults. Wake-word
# detection runs locally (openWakeWord via ONNX Runtime) and needs no account.
# OpenAI API key (Realtime API — speech in, speech out, function calling).
# The SERVER reads this key too, for Bruce's written chat and for indexing the
# books in knowledge/ (`npm run knowledge`) — that half needs no microphone at
# all, so setting OPENAI_API_KEY alone already enables the chat.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# --- Bruce's written chat (server-side; see knowledge/README.md)
# Optional: STARTING chat model (default gpt-5-mini). The picker on the Bruce
# page overrides this and is stored in the database, so this only matters
# before a model has been chosen there.
# BRUCE_CHAT_MODEL=gpt-5-mini
# Optional: reasoning effort for GPT-5 models (default low — keeps replies
# snappy). Set to "" to send no reasoning parameter at all.
# BRUCE_CHAT_REASONING_EFFORT=low
# Optional: embedding model for the knowledge index. Changing it means a
# `npm run knowledge -- --force` rebuild.
# KNOWLEDGE_EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small
# Optional: OpenAI TTS voice (default alloy).
# BRUCE_VOICE=alloy
# Optional: realtime model — gpt-realtime-mini (default) or gpt-realtime.
# BRUCE_REALTIME_MODEL=gpt-realtime-mini
# Optional: the same, for the Talk button on the Bruce page (a phone or laptop
# holding its own voice call) when it should differ from the brewery speaker's.
# BRUCE_VOICE_MODEL=gpt-realtime
# Optional: how a browser call decides you have stopped talking. semantic_vad
# (default) judges it from what you said; server_vad is a plain silence timer,
# worth trying if he cuts in while you think mid-sentence.
# BRUCE_VOICE_VAD=server_vad
# Optional: default speech volume percent (100 = native, up to 200 boost).
# BRUCE_VOLUME_PERCENT=100
# Optional: what the wake phrase triggers before Bruce starts listening —
# speak (says "Yes?"), plop (a short beep), or none (silence). The dashboard's
# Bruce page toggles this live; this sets what he goes back to on restart.
# BRUCE_WAKE_ACK=speak
# Optional: port of Bruce's loopback status API (default 3555); the dashboard
# Bruce page reads it through the server proxy. Set BRUCE_STATUS_URL only if
# Bruce runs on a different machine than the BrewPlanner server.
# BRUCE_STATUS_PORT=3555
# BRUCE_STATUS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3555
# Optional: ALSA capture device for the microphone, e.g. plughw:1.
# Find yours with `arecord -l` once the mic is plugged in.
# BRUCE_MIC_DEVICE=plughw:1
# Optional: the wake phrase. Defaults to the bundled hey_bruce model, so you
# only need this to swap in a different trained .onnx (README-bruce.md).
# BRUCE_WAKE_WORD_MODEL=/home/brewplanner/checklist/apps/bruce/wake-words/hey_bruce.onnx
# Optional: detection score 0..1 (default 0.5) — raise if Bruce wakes on his
# own. Lowering it below 0.5 is not recommended: "hey brew" measures 0.480 and
# starts firing first. BRUCE_WAKE_WORD_DEBUG=1 logs the live scores.
# BRUCE_WAKE_WORD_THRESHOLD=0.5
# BRUCE_WAKE_WORD_DEBUG=1
# Optional: where Bruce persists state (pending reminders). Default ~/.bruce —
# outside the git checkout, so deploys never touch it.
# BRUCE_STATE_DIR=/home/brewplanner/.bruce
# Optional: every tunable in apps/bruce/config.js can be overridden here with
# a BRUCE_ prefix — e.g. silence threshold for a noisy brewery, or barge-in
# (interrupting Bruce mid-speech) once the echo gate is tuned to the room:
# BRUCE_SILENCE_ENERGY_THRESHOLD=350
# BRUCE_BARGE_IN_ENABLED=1
# BRUCE_BARGE_IN_ENERGY_THRESHOLD=400

# --- Recipe backups ---------------------------------------------------------
# The recipe library is written to a JSON file nightly, always locally. These
# settings add a copy in the shared Google Drive folder. Full setup — including
# why a service account needs a Shared Drive and an OAuth token doesn't — is in
# deploy/README-recipe-backup.md.
#
# Option A, works with an ordinary Drive folder (backups owned by your account):
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN=
#
# Option B, headless but needs a Shared Drive (a service account owns no files):
# GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY_FILE=/etc/brewplanner-google.json
#
# Optional: a different destination folder (the id is the last part of its URL).
# GOOGLE_DRIVE_FOLDER_ID=1wYK4s0UCvFI7rQF_WoqhD-Q6DSd03NUC
# Optional: where local copies live, and how many to keep (0 keeps all).
# RECIPE_BACKUP_DIR=/var/lib/brewplanner/recipe-backups
# RECIPE_BACKUP_KEEP=30

# --- Push notifications to the Android app ----------------------------------
# When one account changes something worth knowing about (a setpoint, keg
# contents, a saved recipe, a started brew session, a to-do, a setting), every
# OTHER signed-in phone is notified. You are never told about your own changes.
# Leave unset and the hub simply never pushes. Full setup — Firebase project,
# google-services.json, rebuilding the APK — is in deploy/README-push.md.
#
# The Firebase service-account key (Project settings -> Service accounts ->
# Generate new private key). NOT the same file as google-services.json, and not
# the same key as the Drive one above.
# FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY_FILE=/etc/brewplanner-fcm.json
# Or the JSON inline instead of a path:
# FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY=
